__d(function(e,t,a,i,o,n,r){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:3,chapter_title:"Fibre to Fabric",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a fibre? Give two examples of natural fibres.",back:"A fibre is a thin, thread-like strand that forms the basic unit of textile materials. Natural fibres come from plants or animals. Examples: cotton (from cotton plant seed hair), silk (from silkworm cocoon), jute (from jute plant stem), wool (from sheep fleece).",memory_hook:"Fibre = the world's thinnest raw material. Like a single hair \u2014 many twisted together become yarn, many yarns become fabric."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name two plant fibres and two animal fibres.",back:"Plant fibres: Cotton (from seed hair of cotton plant) and Jute (from stem of jute plant). Also: coir (coconut husk), flax (linen from flax plant stem). Animal fibres: Silk (from silkworm cocoon) and Wool (from sheep fleece). Also: cashmere (from goats), angora (from rabbits).",memory_hook:"Plant fibres = cellulose (like plant cell walls). Animal fibres = protein (like your hair and nails). P for Plant-Cellulose, A for Animal-Protein."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is yarn? How is it different from a fibre?",back:"Yarn is a long, continuous strand made by twisting together many fibres through spinning. A fibre is a single thin strand (too weak and short to use alone). Yarn is the intermediate product \u2014 stronger and longer than individual fibres \u2014 used to weave or knit fabric. Fibre \u2192 Yarn \u2192 Fabric is the sequence.",memory_hook:"Fibre alone = weak. Twist many fibres = Yarn = strong. Like a single thread vs a twisted rope \u2014 the twist gives strength."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is ginning in cotton processing?",back:"Ginning is the process of separating cotton fibres from cotton seeds after the bolls are harvested. A machine called a cotton gin combs and separates the soft white fibres from the seeds. After ginning, the clean fibres are compressed into bales for spinning. Without ginning, the seeds would prevent uniform spinning.",memory_hook:"Gin = separate. Ginning = Giving the seeds and fibres their divorce. Seeds go one way, fluffy fibres go the other."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is retting, and which fibre is obtained using this process?",back:"Retting is the process of soaking jute stalks in slow-moving water for several weeks. The water softens and rots the woody outer layer of the stem, making it easy to separate and extract the jute fibres from inside. After retting, the fibres are stripped out, washed, and dried. Jute fibre is the product obtained by retting.",memory_hook:"Retting = Rotting (in a good way). The water rots just enough of the stem to release the fibre. Jute needs a long bath to give up its threads."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the sequence of steps to convert cotton fibre into woven fabric?",back:"Harvest cotton bolls \u2192 Ginning (separate fibre from seeds) \u2192 Carding (clean and align fibres) \u2192 Spinning (twist fibres into yarn) \u2192 Weaving (interlace yarn on a loom into fabric). This sequence transforms raw cotton plant material into the fabric used in clothing, bedsheets, and towels.",memory_hook:"HGCSW: 'Happy Grandmothers Can Spin Wool' = Harvest, Gin, Card, Spin, Weave."},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is spinning? What was the traditional Indian tool for spinning?",back:"Spinning is the process of drawing out and twisting together clean fibres to form yarn (thread). The twist gives the yarn its strength \u2014 without twisting, the fibres fall apart. The traditional Indian tool for hand spinning is the charkha (spinning wheel), famously used and promoted by Mahatma Gandhi as a symbol of self-reliance during the independence movement.",memory_hook:"Spinning = twisting into strength. Gandhi's charkha = India spinning its own freedom. The spin that made yarn AND made history."},{id:"fc08",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is weaving? What are warp and weft threads?",back:"Weaving is the process of making fabric by interlacing two sets of yarn at right angles on a loom. Warp threads run lengthwise (vertical, stretched along the length of the loom before weaving begins). Weft threads run crosswise (horizontal, passed over and under alternate warp threads using a shuttle). The interlacing creates a stable, grid-like fabric structure.",memory_hook:"Warp = lengthWise (W-A-R-P = runs Along the loom's length). Weft = wanders left-to-right (crosses back and forth). Warp = Wall (vertical). Weft = Way across (horizontal)."},{id:"fc09",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is knitting? How is knitted fabric different from woven fabric?",back:"Knitting is the process of making fabric by using needles to form loops of yarn that interlock with each other. Unlike weaving (which uses two separate sets of threads), knitting uses a single continuous yarn. Key difference: knitted fabrics are stretchy (elastic) because loops can expand; woven fabrics are less stretchy because threads are locked at right angles. Sweaters, socks, and T-shirts are knitted.",memory_hook:"Knitting = loops of love (like a chain of linked rings). Weaving = grid (like a window screen). Loops stretch, grids don't."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is sericulture? Where does silk thread come from?",back:"Sericulture is the practice of rearing silkworms (Bombyx mori) to produce silk. The silkworm larva feeds on mulberry leaves and then spins a continuous protein thread around itself to form a protective cocoon. One cocoon can yield up to 1,000\u20131,500 metres of continuous silk thread. To harvest, cocoons are steamed or boiled, and the thread is carefully unwound.",memory_hook:"Sericulture = Silk + Culture (farming). The silkworm is nature's textile factory \u2014 it spins its own sleeping bag made of silk."},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is Karnataka called the silk capital of India?",back:"Karnataka produces approximately 40% of India's total silk, making it the nation's largest silk-producing state. The Ramanagara district hosts Asia's second-largest silk cocoon market. The world-famous Mysore silk saree \u2014 a Geographical Indication (GI)-tagged product \u2014 is made from Karnataka silk with pure zari (gold thread). Sericulture employs lakhs of farmers in Ramanagara, Kolar, Mysuru, and Tumakuru districts.",memory_hook:"Karnataka = 40% of India's silk = undisputed Silk King of India. Ramanagara = where Asia's silkworms go to market."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why are cotton clothes comfortable to wear in summer while woollen clothes are warm in winter?",back:"Cotton is a plant fibre (cellulose) that absorbs moisture (sweat) and allows air to circulate \u2014 keeping the body cool in summer. Wool is an animal fibre (protein/keratin) with a natural crimp (curl) that traps air between the fibres, creating an insulating layer that retains body heat \u2014 keeping the body warm in winter. The physical structure of each fibre determines its thermal properties.",memory_hook:"Cotton = Cool conductor (absorbs sweat, breathes). Wool = Warm wrapper (traps air, insulates). C for Cool-Cotton, W for Warm-Wool."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why are jute bags considered more environmentally friendly than plastic bags?",back:"Jute is a natural plant fibre \u2014 it is biodegradable (breaks down naturally in soil within months) and renewable (jute plants grow quickly). Plastic bags are made from synthetic polymers derived from petroleum \u2014 they take hundreds of years to decompose and release harmful microplastics. Jute bags can be reused many times and, when discarded, cause no lasting environmental harm.",memory_hook:"Jute bag = green choice. It grew from the earth and returns to the earth. Plastic bag = earth's enemy \u2014 it never leaves."},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does twisting fibres during spinning make yarn stronger?",back:"Individual fibres are short and weak \u2014 they slide past each other easily. When fibres are twisted together during spinning, friction between the fibres increases dramatically. The twisted structure also creates a mechanical interlocking between fibres. Together, friction and interlocking prevent the fibres from sliding apart, giving the yarn strength proportional to the tightness of the twist.",memory_hook:"Twist = strength. Like a rope \u2014 individual strands are weak, but twisted together they hold a ship. Spinning transfers the same principle to tiny fibres."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Look at your school uniform shirt. How would you determine whether the fabric is woven or knitted?",back:"Method 1 (visual): Look closely at the fabric structure. Woven fabric shows a grid of horizontal and vertical threads; knitted fabric shows interlocking loops. Method 2 (stretch test): Pull gently. Woven fabric resists stretching (threads are locked). Knitted fabric stretches easily (loops expand). Most formal shirts are woven cotton; most T-shirts and jerseys are knitted cotton.",memory_hook:"Grid = woven (like window mesh). Loops = knitted (like a chain-link fence). Stretch test = the quickest way to know."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Ilkal saree and why is it significant to this chapter?",back:"The Ilkal saree is a famous handwoven saree from Ilkal town in Bagalkot district, Karnataka. It has a distinctive contrasting pallu (border section), traditionally woven with a mixture of cotton and silk yarn. Ilkal weavers use traditional handlooms \u2014 demonstrating the full fibre-to-fabric process (spinning yarn, threading the loom, weaving warp and weft). It is a GI-tagged Karnataka textile product.",memory_hook:"Ilkal = India's handloom heritage in one saree. Every thread woven by hand = Chapter 3 in real life."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"From which part of the cotton plant is cotton fibre obtained?",back:"Cotton fibre is obtained from the seed hair (trichome) of the cotton plant. After the cotton flower is pollinated, it forms a boll \u2014 a capsule containing seeds surrounded by long, soft white fibres. When the boll matures and bursts open, the fluffy white fibres (attached to the seeds) are visible and ready for harvesting.",memory_hook:"Cotton comes from the seed's soft coat \u2014 like the cotton candy cloud wrapped around a tiny seed. The plant's seed wraps itself in fibre, and we harvest that wrapping."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Arrange these steps in the correct order: Weaving, Spinning, Ginning, Harvesting cotton bolls, Carding.",back:"Correct order: (1) Harvesting cotton bolls \u2192 (2) Ginning (separate fibre from seeds) \u2192 (3) Carding (clean and align fibres) \u2192 (4) Spinning (twist fibres into yarn) \u2192 (5) Weaving (interlace yarn into fabric). Each step transforms the raw material \u2014 plant \u2192 fibre \u2192 aligned fibre \u2192 yarn \u2192 fabric.",memory_hook:"HGCSW: 'Happy Grandmothers Can Spin Well' = Harvest \u2192 Gin \u2192 Card \u2192 Spin \u2192 Weave. Same order, every time."},{id:"fc19",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is Mahatma Gandhi associated with the charkha (spinning wheel)?",back:"Gandhi promoted the charkha (hand spinning wheel) as a tool of political and economic resistance against British colonial rule. Britain imported raw cotton from India cheaply, manufactured cloth in British mills, and sold the expensive finished cloth back to India. Gandhi urged Indians to spin their own yarn and weave their own cloth (khadi), making India economically self-reliant and boycotting British textiles. The charkha became the symbol of the Swadeshi movement.",memory_hook:"Charkha = spin your freedom. Gandhi turned a simple spinning wheel into a political weapon against colonial exploitation."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A student says silk is a plant product because silkworms eat mulberry leaves (plants). Is this correct? Why or why not?",back:"This is incorrect. Silk is classified as an animal product because it is produced BY an animal (the silkworm/silkworm larva). The silkworm eats mulberry leaves (plant), but the silk thread itself is a protein secreted by the silkworm's silk glands \u2014 it is the animal's own biological product. Classification is based on who makes the product, not what the maker eats. Silkworm = animal \u2192 silk = animal product.",memory_hook:"Same logic as honey: bees eat nectar (plant) but honey = animal product. Silkworms eat leaves (plant) but silk = animal product. It is the maker that counts."}]}},2898,[]);